  "readme": [
    {
      "section": "dsh-egress-gate",
      "text": "> **If this helps you, please give it a star** — it helps others find it. Thanks!\n\nDeterministic network egress gate for\n[DeepSeek Harness](https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness) (dsh)."
    },
    {
      "section": "dsh-egress-gate",
      "text": "Content scanning (dsh-read-guard) guards what **enters** the model; this\nguards where data may **leave** to. `web_fetch` / `web_search` calls are\nchecked against a destination allowlist in `tools/pre-execute` and denied\nbefore they run. Shell commands are scanned for fetch-verb URLs —\nobservation by default, enforcement opt-in, honest about precision. Zero LLM\ncalls in the trust path.\n\nFamily: read · write · do · call · leave-behind · **egress (where data may\nflow)**."
    },
    {
      "section": "Why now (checked 2026-10-12)",
      "text": "Empty lane. Five keyword searches (`network allowlist`, `egress firewall`,\n`url filter`, `web fetch restrict`, …) return nothing on GitHub; the awesome\nlist has no entry gating destinations. Neighbours are all different axes:\ncontent-pattern secret rules (dsh-defend), LLM-judged commands\n(dsh-jev-guard), proxy *configuration* (official network-proxy docs). For\nteams, \"which hosts may the agent reach\" is the first compliance question —\nand the deterministic answer was unowned."
    },
    {
      "section": "Policy",
      "text": "- Deny always wins; a non-empty `allow` list means \"only these hosts\"\n  (`example.com` covers `api.example.com`; `*.example.com` is the explicit\n  form).\n- Web tools are enforced at full precision — the URL is in the arguments.\n- Shell: URLs following `curl` / `wget` / `Invoke-WebRequest` /\n  `Invoke-RestMethod` / `fetch` / `http` on the same command segment. This\n  cannot see through scripts, variables, or proxies — so it warns by\n  default and denies only when you opt into `shellMode: 'enforce'`."
    },
    {
      "section": "Install",
      "text": "```sh\ndsh plugin --profile default add github:zhengqiuyang/dsh-egress-gate\n```\n\nPrebuilt `lib/` ships in the repo — git installs need no build step and no\n`allowBuilds` authorization."
    },
    {
      "section": "Configure",
      "text": "```yaml\n- id: dsh-egress-gate\n  name: dsh-egress-gate\n  config:\n    webTools: [web_fetch, web_search]"
    },
    {
      "section": "Compatibility",
      "text": "- **DSH**: verified against the 0.2.x line (source snapshot 0.2.1-alpha.2); installs on any profile via `dsh plugin add` (prebuilt `lib/`, no build approval).\n- **Node**: >= 20 (CI matrix: Node 20/22/24 on Ubuntu and Windows).\n- **Disposable-profile evidence (2026-10-19)**: `dsh plugin --profile <fresh> add github:zhengqiuyang/dsh-egress-gate` → installed in 30s; boot shows `[dsh-egress-gate] active: …`; `dsh plugin --profile <fresh> remove dsh-egress-gate` → dependencies cleared, bundles restored."
    },
    {
      "section": "Develop",
      "text": "```sh\nnpm install && npm test\n```"
    },
    {
      "section": "Kill criteria",
      "text": "If dsh ships first-party destination policy, or proxy-layer plugins add\nper-host rules, this freezes at its last working version with a postmortem\nhere."
    },
    {
      "section": "Known limitations",
      "text": "- **Web tools are enforced; shell is observe-only by default** (set `shellMode: 'enforce'` to change). A fetch verb gates every URL in its own command segment; segments without one (a plain echo) are not treated as sends.\n- **No redirect resolution**: the check sees the requested host, not where a redirect lands.\n- **This is not an OS-level network control.** Scripts, sockets, proxies, and MCP tools with their own network access are outside this gate — use a sandbox or proxy for hard boundaries."
    }
  ],
